    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# 2.03 — Cross-Ratio and Projectivities\n\nIn 2.01 we watched invariants die as the group grew: distance went first, then ratio, and one quantity survived everything. This notebook is about that survivor.\n\nA **projectivity** of $\\mathbb{RP}^2$ is a map induced by an invertible linear map of $\\mathbb R^3$:\n\n$$[x] \\longmapsto [Mx], \\qquad M \\in GL_3(\\mathbb R).$$\n\nSince $M$ and $\\lambda M$ induce the same map on classes, the group is\n\n$$PGL_3(\\mathbb R) = GL_3(\\mathbb R)/\\{\\lambda I\\}, \\qquad \\dim = 9 - 1 = 8.$$\n\nTwo facts organise everything:\n\n**The fundamental theorem.** $PGL(3)$ acts *simply transitively* on ordered 4-tuples of points in general position. Given any two such 4-tuples there is exactly one projectivity carrying the first to the second. This is what licensed the normalisation in 2.02's Desargues proof, and we now prove it by construction.\n\n**The cross-ratio.** Four points *on a line* are a different matter. Three collinear points can be sent anywhere by a projectivity of that line, but four cannot — a single number obstructs it:\n\n$$(P_1, P_2; P_3, P_4) = \\frac{(x_3 - x_1)(x_4 - x_2)}{(x_3 - x_2)(x_4 - x_1)}.$$\n\nThe cross-ratio is the complete invariant of four collinear points, and by duality also of four concurrent lines. It is the reason perspective drawings can be measured, why a photograph of a building determines the building's proportions, and — via 2.05–2.06 — why hyperbolic distance has the form it does.\n\nThe counting is worth doing now: a projectivity of a line has 3 parameters ($PGL_2$ has dimension $4-1=3$), so it can move 3 points freely and no more. The fourth point's position relative to the first three is forced, and the cross-ratio is exactly that forced datum.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 1. The fundamental theorem of projective geometry\n\n> **Theorem.** Let $A, B, C, D$ and $A', B', C', D'$ be two 4-tuples of points of $\\mathbb{RP}^2$, each in **general position** (no three collinear). Then there is a **unique** projectivity taking $A \\mapsto A'$, $B \\mapsto B'$, $C \\mapsto C'$, $D \\mapsto D'$.\n\nThe proof is a construction, and it is more useful than the statement.\n\n**Step 1 — everything reduces to the standard frame.** It suffices to send an arbitrary frame to\n$$E_1 = [1{:}0{:}0], \\quad E_2 = [0{:}1{:}0], \\quad E_3 = [0{:}0{:}1], \\quad U = [1{:}1{:}1],$$\nbecause we can then compose one such map with the inverse of another.\n\n**Step 2 — solve for the scalars.** Take representative vectors $a, b, c, d \\in \\mathbb R^3$. Since no three of the points are collinear, $\\{a,b,c\\}$ is a basis, so\n$$d = \\alpha a + \\beta b + \\gamma c$$\nfor unique scalars, all nonzero (a zero coefficient would put $D$ on a line through two of the others). Now set\n$$M = \\bigl[\\,\\alpha a \\;\\big|\\; \\beta b \\;\\big|\\; \\gamma c\\,\\bigr]^{-1}.$$\nThen $M$ sends $\\alpha a \\mapsto e_1$, so $A \\mapsto E_1$, and similarly for $B, C$; and $d = \\alpha a + \\beta b + \\gamma c \\mapsto e_1 + e_2 + e_3$, so $D \\mapsto U$.\n\n**Step 3 — uniqueness.** Any projectivity fixing all four of $E_1, E_2, E_3, U$ has a matrix that is diagonal (to fix each $E_i$) with equal entries (to fix $U$), hence a scalar — the identity in $PGL$.\n\nNotice why *four* points are needed rather than three. Three points fix the matrix only up to the diagonal scalings $\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma)$ — a 2-parameter ambiguity in $PGL$. The fourth point is precisely what removes it. This is the same $8 = 9 - 1$ dimension count as before: $4 \\times 2 = 8$ conditions, $8$ parameters.\n\nLet us build the map.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ncross3(a,b) := [a[2]*b[3]-a[3]*b[2], a[3]*b[1]-a[1]*b[3], a[1]*b[2]-a[2]*b[1]]$\ndet3(a,b,c) := determinant(matrix(a,b,c))$\njoin(P,Q)   := cross3(P,Q)$\nmeet(l,m)   := cross3(l,m)$\ncvec(v)     := transpose(matrix(v))$\npt(M)       := args(transpose(M))[1]$\n\nnormalise(v) := block([k],\n  k : 0, for i : 1 thru length(v) do if v[i] # 0 then k : i,\n  if k = 0 then error(\"zero vector is not a projective point\"),\n  ratsimp(v / v[k]) )$\nsame_point(u,v) := is( normalise(u) = normalise(v) )$\n\n/* Map a frame (A,B,C,D) in general position to the standard frame E1,E2,E3,U */\nto_standard(A, B, C, D) := block([Mabc, coeffs, α, β, γ],\n  Mabc   : transpose(matrix(A, B, C)),      /* columns a, b, c */\n  coeffs : pt( invert(Mabc) . cvec(D) ),    /* d = αa + βb + γc */\n  [α, β, γ] : coeffs,\n  if α = 0 or β = 0 or γ = 0\n    then error(\"points are not in general position\"),\n  invert( transpose(matrix(α*A, β*B, γ*C)) ) )$\n\n/* The projectivity carrying one frame onto another */\nframe_map(F, G) := ratsimp( invert(to_standard(G[1],G[2],G[3],G[4]))\n                            . to_standard(F[1],F[2],F[3],F[4]) )$\n\napply_proj(M, P) := pt( M . cvec(P) )$\n\n/* --- Test: an arbitrary frame to the standard one --- */\nA : [1, 2, 1]$  B : [3, -1, 1]$  C : [0, 4, 1]$  D : [2, 2, 1]$\nT : to_standard(A, B, C, D)$\nprint(\"T·A = \", normalise(apply_proj(T, A)))$\nprint(\"T·B = \", normalise(apply_proj(T, B)))$\nprint(\"T·C = \", normalise(apply_proj(T, C)))$\nprint(\"T·D = \", normalise(apply_proj(T, D)), \"   ← the unit point\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 12,
      "outputs": [
