    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 2. The cross-ratio\n\nOn a *line*, the group available is $PGL_2(\\mathbb R)$, of dimension $4 - 1 = 3$. So a projectivity of a line can carry any three distinct points to any other three — but the fourth point is then determined, and what pins it down is the cross-ratio.\n\n**Affine form.** For four points with parameters $x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4$,\n\n$$(P_1,P_2;P_3,P_4) = \\frac{(x_3-x_1)(x_4-x_2)}{(x_3-x_2)(x_4-x_1)}.$$\n\n**Projective form.** Better, because it needs no coordinate on the line. The four points span a 2-dimensional subspace of $\\mathbb R^3$; take $P_1, P_2$ as a basis and write\n\n$$P_3 = a P_1 + b P_2, \\qquad P_4 = c P_1 + d P_2 .$$\n\nThen\n\n$$(P_1,P_2;P_3,P_4) = \\frac{b\\,c}{a\\,d},$$\n\nwhich is manifestly unchanged by any linear map (it is a ratio of coordinates in a basis that transforms with the points) and by any rescaling of the representatives (each of $a,b,c,d$ picks up a factor that cancels).\n\nThat second form is the honest one: it shows the cross-ratio is projective by construction, rather than by a computation that happens to work out.\n\n**Degenerate values carry meaning.** The cross-ratio takes the values $0, 1, \\infty$ exactly when two of the four points coincide, so on genuinely distinct points it lands in $\\mathbb R \\setminus \\{0,1\\}$. And the value $-1$ is special enough to have a name: the four points are then a **harmonic set**, $P_3$ and $P_4$ being *harmonic conjugates* with respect to $P_1, P_2$. Harmonic sets are constructible with a straightedge alone, which makes them the projective analogue of the midpoint — and indeed if $P_4$ is at infinity, harmonic conjugacy says exactly that $P_3$ is the midpoint of $P_1P_2$.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Affine cross-ratio of four parameters on a line */\ncr_affine(x1,x2,x3,x4) := ratsimp( ((x3-x1)*(x4-x2)) / ((x3-x2)*(x4-x1)) )$\n\n/* Solve P = s·P1 + t·P2 for (s,t), using whichever pair of coordinate rows\n   is independent.  P1, P2, P must be collinear in RP² for this to be exact. */\ncoeffs_in_basis(P1, P2, P) := block([i, j, D, found, s, t],\n  found : false,\n  for i : 1 thru 3 do\n    for j : i+1 thru 3 do\n      if not found then block([],\n        D : ratsimp( P1[i]*P2[j] - P1[j]*P2[i] ),\n        if D # 0 then (\n          s : ratsimp(( P[i]*P2[j] - P[j]*P2[i] )/D),\n          t : ratsimp(( P1[i]*P[j] - P1[j]*P[i] )/D),\n          found : true )),\n  if not found then error(\"P1 and P2 are the same projective point\"),\n  [s, t] )$\n\n/* Projective cross-ratio: P3 = a P1 + b P2, P4 = c P1 + d P2, ratio = bc/(ad) */\ncr(P1, P2, P3, P4) := block([u, v],\n  u : coeffs_in_basis(P1, P2, P3),\n  v : coeffs_in_basis(P1, P2, P4),\n  ratsimp( (u[2]*v[1]) / (u[1]*v[2]) ) )$\n\n/* The two formulas agree, for symbolic points on a line */\np1 : [x1,1,0]$  p2 : [x2,1,0]$  p3 : [x3,1,0]$  p4 : [x4,1,0]$\nprint(\"affine formula    : \", cr_affine(x1,x2,x3,x4))$\nprint(\"projective formula: \", cr(p1,p2,p3,p4))$\nprint(\"difference        : \", ratsimp( cr_affine(x1,x2,x3,x4) - cr(p1,p2,p3,p4) ))$\n\n/* Degenerate values mark coincidences */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"cross-ratio when P3 = P1 : \", cr_affine(x1,x2,x1,x4), \"   (0)\")$\nprint(\"cross-ratio when P3 = P2 : \", \"undefined — the denominator vanishes (∞)\")$\nprint(\"cross-ratio when P4 = P3 : \", ratsimp(cr_affine(x1,x2,x3,x3)), \"   (1)\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 14,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "affine formula    :  ((x3-x1)*x4-x2*x3+x1*x2)/((x3-x2)*x4-x1*x3+x1*x2)\nprojective formula:  ((x3-x1)*x4-x2*x3+x1*x2)/((x3-x2)*x4-x1*x3+x1*x2)\ndifference        :  0\ncross-ratio when P3 = P1 :  0    (0)\ncross-ratio when P3 = P2 :  undefined — the denominator vanishes (∞)\ncross-ratio when P4 = P3 :  1    (1)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- Invariance under a projectivity of the line --- */\n/* A projectivity of a line acts on the affine parameter as a Möbius map */\nmoeb(t) := (a*t + b)/(c*t + d)$\n\nlhs_cr : cr_affine( moeb(x1), moeb(x2), moeb(x3), moeb(x4) )$\nprint(\"cross-ratio of the images = \", factor(lhs_cr))$\nprint(\"cross-ratio of the originals = \", factor(cr_affine(x1,x2,x3,x4)))$\nprint(\"difference = \", ratsimp( lhs_cr - cr_affine(x1,x2,x3,x4) ), \"  — invariant ∎\")$\n\n/* Distance and ratio are NOT invariant, as 2.01 found */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"ratio (x3−x1)/(x3−x2) of images vs originals:\")$\nprint(\"   difference = \",\n      ratsimp( (moeb(x3)-moeb(x1))/(moeb(x3)-moeb(x2)) - (x3-x1)/(x3-x2) ))$\nprint(\"   — nonzero unless c = 0, i.e. unless the map is affine\")$\n\n/* --- Invariance in full generality: an arbitrary projectivity of RP² --- */\nMg : matrix([2,1,-1],[0,3,1],[1,-1,2])$\nq1 : [0,0,1]$  q2 : [1,0,1]$  q3 : [2,0,1]$  q4 : [5,0,1]$   /* four points on y = 0 */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"four collinear points, cross-ratio        = \", cr(q1,q2,q3,q4))$\nprint(\"their images under a general projectivity = \",\n      cr( apply_proj(Mg,q1), apply_proj(Mg,q2),\n          apply_proj(Mg,q3), apply_proj(Mg,q4) ))$\nprint(\"images are still collinear? \",\n      is( ratsimp(det3(apply_proj(Mg,q1), apply_proj(Mg,q2), apply_proj(Mg,q3))) = 0 ))$",
      "metadata": {},
