    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 3. Reordering the four points\n\nThe cross-ratio depends on the *order* of the four points. There are $4! = 24$ orderings, but only **six** distinct values, because the cross-ratio is unchanged by the four permutations forming a Klein four-group (swapping both pairs, or swapping the pairs with each other). The quotient $S_4/V_4 \\cong S_3$ acts on the value, producing\n\n$$\\lambda, \\quad \\frac1\\lambda, \\quad 1-\\lambda, \\quad \\frac{1}{1-\\lambda}, \\quad \\frac{\\lambda}{\\lambda-1}, \\quad \\frac{\\lambda-1}{\\lambda}.$$\n\nThis six-element group is classically called the **anharmonic group**. Two of its orbits are exceptionally small, and both are famous:\n\n- $\\lambda = -1, \\tfrac12, 2$ — the orbit of size 3, the **harmonic** case.\n- $\\lambda = -\\omega, -\\omega^2$ where $\\omega = e^{2\\pi i/3}$ — the orbit of size 2, the **equianharmonic** case, which is complex and reappears in the theory of elliptic curves (the $j$-invariant is built from exactly this).\n\nLet the computation find the six values rather than taking them on trust.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Take four points whose cross-ratio is the symbol λ, and permute them.\n   Solve (x3−x1)(x4−x2) / ((x3−x2)(x4−x1)) = λ with x1,x2,x3 pinned at 0,1,∞-free\n   choices: use 0, 1, and a free x4 chosen to realise λ. */\nbase : [0, 1, %lam, 2]$   /* placeholder, replaced below */\n\n/* Simplest realisation: x1 = 0, x2 = 1, x3 = ∞ is awkward affinely, so instead\n   pick x1 = 0, x2 = 1, x4 = 2 and solve for x3 giving cross-ratio λ. */\nx3sol : rhs( solve( cr_affine(0, 1, xx, 2) = lam, xx )[1] )$\nquad  : [0, 1, x3sol, 2]$\nprint(\"base 4-tuple with cross-ratio λ: \", quad)$\nprint(\"check: \", ratsimp( cr_affine(quad[1],quad[2],quad[3],quad[4]) ))$\n\n/* All 24 orderings; collect the distinct values as functions of λ */\nperms : permutations([1,2,3,4])$\nvals  : []$\nfor p in perms do block([v],\n  v : ratsimp( cr_affine(quad[p[1]], quad[p[2]], quad[p[3]], quad[p[4]]) ),\n  if not member(v, vals) then vals : cons(v, vals) )$\nvals : sort(vals)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"distinct values among all 24 orderings: \", length(vals))$\nfor v in vals do print(\"    \", v)$\n\n/* The harmonic orbit: λ = −1 */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"at λ = −1 the six values collapse to: \",\n      sort(unique( map(lambda([v], ratsimp(subst(lam = -1, v))), vals) )))$\nprint(\"— an orbit of size 3: the harmonic case\")$\nprint(\"at λ = 2   they are: \",\n      sort(unique( map(lambda([v], ratsimp(subst(lam = 2, v))), vals) )))$\nprint(\"— the same orbit, so λ = −1, 1/2, 2 all describe a harmonic set\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 16,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "base 4-tuple with cross-ratio λ:  [0,1,(2*lam)/(2*lam-1),2]\ncheck:  lam\ndistinct values among all 24 orderings:  6\n     -(1/(lam-1))\n     1/lam\n     (lam-1)/lam\n     1-lam\n     lam\n     lam/(lam-1)\nat λ = −1 the six values collapse to:  [-1,1/2,2]\n— an orbit of size 3: the harmonic case\nat λ = 2   they are:  [-1,1/2,2]\n— the same orbit, so λ = −1, 1/2, 2 all describe a harmonic set"
          ]
        }
      ]
