      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n| Result | Statement |\n|---|---|\n| **Fundamental theorem** | $PGL(3)$ acts simply transitively on 4-point frames in general position |\n| **Cross-ratio** | the complete invariant of 4 collinear points, $\\dfrac{(x_3-x_1)(x_4-x_2)}{(x_3-x_2)(x_4-x_1)}$ |\n| **Invariance** | preserved by every projectivity; the *ratio* fails by a term proportional to $c$ |\n| **Six values** | reordering gives $\\lambda, \\frac1\\lambda, 1-\\lambda, \\frac{1}{1-\\lambda}, \\frac{\\lambda}{\\lambda-1}, \\frac{\\lambda-1}{\\lambda}$ |\n| **Harmonic set** | $\\lambda = -1$; constructible with straightedge alone |\n| **Midpoint** | the harmonic conjugate of the point at infinity |\n\nThe dimension counting told the whole story in advance. $PGL_2$ has 3 parameters, so it moves 3 points freely and no more; the fourth point's position is the one datum left over, and that datum is the cross-ratio. $PGL_3$ has 8 parameters and 4 points impose 8 conditions, so a plane projectivity is pinned down exactly — no invariant at all for 4 points in general position, which is why the invariant lives on *lines* rather than in the plane.\n\nTwo computations are worth remembering:\n\n**The failure of ratio was proportional to $c$.** In $x \\mapsto (ax+b)/(cx+d)$, setting $c = 0$ gives an affine map and restores the ratio. So \"affine\" is exactly \"projective, fixing the point at infinity\" — the 1-dimensional shadow of 1.05's claim, now verified rather than asserted.\n\n**The midpoint is a projective notion in disguise.** Straightedge alone cannot bisect a segment, but it can construct the harmonic conjugate of any point. Bisection is what harmonic conjugacy becomes once you nominate a line at infinity. The metric idea was projective all along; the metric only chose *which* projective configuration to call the middle.\n\n## Next\n\n**2.04 — Conics Projectively** applies the fundamental theorem to conics. In 1.06 there were ellipses, parabolas and hyperbolas, distinguished by eigenvalue signs. Projectively there is only **one** non-degenerate conic — the three Euclidean types differ solely in how the curve meets the line at infinity — and five points determine it.\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Stillwell, J. (2005) *The Four Pillars of Geometry*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/0-387-29052-4. §5.6 (linear fractional functions), §5.7 (the cross-ratio) and §5.8 (\"What is special about the cross-ratio\") cover this notebook almost exactly.\n- ★ Hartshorne, R. (2000) *Geometry: Euclid and Beyond*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-0-387-22676-7. Introduces the cross-ratio at p. 344 — inside the non-Euclidean chapter, where it is used to *define* hyperbolic distance. That is the route 2.06 will take.\n- ★ Lee, N.-H. (2020) *Geometry: from Isometries to Special Relativity*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-030-42101-4. The cross-ratio under the Möbius group, feeding into 2.05.\n- Richter-Gebert, J. (2011) *Perspectives on Projective Geometry*. Berlin: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-642-17286-1. The fundamental theorem and the cross-ratio, developed computationally with the same meet/join calculus used here.\n- Coxeter, H.S.M. (1987) *Projective Geometry*. 2nd edn. New York: Springer. The classical synthetic treatment of harmonic conjugates and the complete quadrangle.\n\nOn the equianharmonic case and its role in the $j$-invariant of elliptic curves, see ★ Hartshorne, R. (1977) *Algebraic Geometry*. Graduate Texts in Mathematics 52. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-3849-0, Chapter IV — well beyond this journey, and a signpost only.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
